# Chapter 12: Linux Kernel Memory Management — Slab Allocators, Page Tables, and NUMA Balancing

## 12.1 Introduction

The Linux kernel is, at its core, a memory manager. Every process, every file cache entry, every network buffer, and every device driver consumes memory that the kernel must allocate, track, and reclaim. Unlike user-space allocators (which can lean on the kernel for backing), the kernel must be self-sufficient: it cannot page its own metadata to disk without risking deadlock. This chapter examines three interlocking subsystems: the **slab allocator** (the kernel's general-purpose object cache), the **page table** structures that map virtual to physical memory, and the **NUMA balancing** machinery that keeps memory access local to the CPU that uses it.

We will move from the smallest unit (the page frame) upward through the slab caches, into the page-table hierarchy, and finally to the system-wide NUMA policy engine. Code examples are drawn from the mainline kernel (6.x series) and are simplified for pedagogy but faithful to the real interfaces.

## 12.2 The Physical Page Frame: The Atom of Kernel Memory

The kernel views physical memory as an array of **page frames**, each typically 4 KiB on x86-64 (configurable to 64 KiB on some architectures). Each frame is described by a `struct page`:

```c
struct page {
    unsigned long flags;      /* PG_locked, PG_dirty, PG_slab, ... */
    atomic_t _refcount;
    union {
        struct {                    /* when on LRU lists */
            struct list_head lru;
            struct address_space *mapping;
            unsigned long index;
        };
        struct {                    /* when PG_slab */
            struct kmem_cache *slab_cache;
            void *freelist;
            union { struct list_head partial; };
        };
        /* ... many other unions ... */
    };
};
```

The `flags` word encodes the frame's state. `PG_slab`, for instance, tells the page-frame allociator that this frame is owned by a slab cache and must not be returned to the buddy allociator directly. The `_refcount` is the number of references (page-table entries, kernel mappings, pin operations) currently held.

Frames are managed by the **buddy allociator**, which tracks free memory in power-of-two blocks (orders 0 through `MAX_ORDER`, usually 10 or 11). A request for `2^n` contiguous frames is satisfied by splitting a larger free block; freeing coalesces buddies back together. The budy allociator is the substrate on which slab caches sit.

